WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.050
- [Narrator] Take a
look at these two guns.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.590
One is an AR-15 Style Rifle,

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.080
that's been altered with something called

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.000
a Juggernaut Tactical Hellfighter Kit.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.763
The other is a classic Colt AR-15.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.910
The two guns look kind of different.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.160
But they have a lot of the same features.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.200
And a lot of similarities
in how they shoot.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.740
The biggest difference
you actually can't see.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.560
One has been illegal in California

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.260
for more than 30 years, which
is why we had to use a photo.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.543
The other you can buy
at a local gun store.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.420
Over the past few decades
lawmakers in California

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.020
have repeatedly tried to ban

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.328
what they define as assault weapons.

00:00:39.328 --> 00:00:42.350
And each time gun manufacturers responded

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.700
by making small changes
so they could continue

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.783
selling that style of gun in the state.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.160
One of these guns, an AR-15 style rifle

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.850
was used in April in a shooting

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.493
at a synagogue near San Diego.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.410
Despite California's bans,
the gun the shooter was using

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.180
was perfectly legal in the state,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.700
according to law enforcement officials

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.110
familiar with the investigation.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.700
To understand how this happened,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.533
you have to look at how guns
in the state have evolved.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.740
First here's the Colt AR-15 again.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.330
This is approximately
what AR-15s looked like

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.260
back before the state's first ban in 1989.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.060
And what they still look like in states

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.050
that haven't passed bans.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.310
It has a pistol grip
and a flash suppressor,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.760
features that California
lawmakers have since said

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.780
make the weapons particularly dangerous.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.320
This type of gun was banned in 1989

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.760
after a gunman entered a school

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.410
in Stockton, California, and opened fire.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:40.711
He killed five, and injured
32 others, using an AK-47.

00:01:40.711 --> 00:01:43.600
After that, the state
passed the Roberti-Roos

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.460
Assault Weapons Control Act,

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.243
which bans certain guns by name.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:51.110
- There was a lot of outrage,
and this was the impetus

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.210
for the first assault weapons ban

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.240
in the nation, in California.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.110
- [Narrator] In response,
gunmakers started

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.950
developing similar guns
with different names.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.190
That meant that guns like the Colt AR-15

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.240
were changed slightly and resold

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.890
with names like the Colt Sporter.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.260
Then, in 1999, lawmakers amended the law

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.820
to ban weapons with specific parts.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:16.270
Such as semi-automatic rifles
with detachable magazines,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.600
pistol grips, thumbhole
stocks, folding stocks,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.020
grenade launcher, flash suppressors,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.190
and forward pistol grips.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:25.840
But there was a way around this.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.000
Guns with fixed magazines
could keep these features.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.410
- One of the areas they
focused on was making it

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.320
harder for mass shooters to reload

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.380
and have as many rounds of
ammunition in their magazine.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.620
The definition of a fixed magazine

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.590
was that you couldn't
remove it without a tool.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.790
And the manufacturers realized that

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.630
a tool could be a bullet.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.580
- [Narrator] So gunmakers
modified guns so the magazines

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.460
could be changed with a single bullet,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.663
which met the state's
definition of a tool.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.920
This allowed them to
keep the banned features

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.910
and still sell guns with magazines

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.690
that could be changed
without much trouble.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.890
This became known as the
Bullet Button Loophole.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.430
This bullet button modification

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:13.133
was legal in California
for around a decade.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.190
Then, in 2015, two shooters
stormed a holiday gathering

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.483
in San Bernardino, California,
and killed 14 people.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.760
They were carrying AR-15 style rifles

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.020
with bullet buttons they
had bought in California

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.403
and then illegally altered.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.880
After the shooting, lawmakers
tried to pass new legislation.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.560
This time they focused on closing

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.933
the Bullet Button Loophole.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.220
- This incident caught the attention

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.670
of a state lawmaker by
the name of Steve Glazer.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.890
And he decides he wants to try to close

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.560
this so-called Bullet Button Loophole.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.020
And so he does something to
ban these bullet button guns,

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.850
and what he does is changes the definition

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:57.497
of a fixed magazine to
one that cannot be removed

00:03:57.497 --> 00:04:01.330
without the disassembly
of the firearm action.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.750
- [Narrator] The law passed, which meant

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.760
bullet button guns were banned.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.880
And yet again modifications were made

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.760
so that more AR style
features could be kept.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.570
Remember the AR style rifle with

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.130
the Juggernaut Tactical
Hellfighter from the beginning?

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.770
The Juggernaut Tactical Hellfighter

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.070
is a kit that alters
an assault style rifle

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.060
so that users can change the magazine

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.733
by cracking open the action
just a hair, like this.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.950
Gun dealers say this legally counts

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.283
as disassembly of the firearm action.

00:04:31.135 --> 00:04:33.020
So gunmakers started here.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.100
And decades later, after
multiple assault weapon bans,

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.510
they ended here.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.890
- If you talk to any gun dealer
or person who shoots guns,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.160
they'll tell you a
California compliant AR-15,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:49.160
as they are called, shoots just
the same as a normal AR-15.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.443
The bullet's going just as fast.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:55.010
- [Narrator] Smith and Wesson,
Ruger, Colt, and Juggernaut

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.340
did not respond to a request for comment.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:00.030
A spokesperson from the National
Shooting Sports Foundation,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.210
a trade group for gun manufacturers,

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.980
said of the state laws,
"They're basing the bans

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.750
strictly on cosmetic
features that have no bearing

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.800
on the operation or the
function of the firearm."

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.490
A spokesperson for the
California state attorney general

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:15.853
declined to comment on
the modified weapons.

